usb: ehci — bring up the CE5300/CE2600 TransDimension host controller

Every USB port on the EA is dead under mainline: all three EHCI controllers
(PCI 192e:0101, a TransDimension/TDI core, rev 07) fail at probe with

    ehci-pci 0000:01:0d.0: can't setup: -110
    ehci-pci 0000:01:0d.0: probe with driver ehci-pci failed with error -110

-110 is -ETIMEDOUT: ehci_setup()'s reset handshake never completes. The silicon
is fine -- post-failure its registers read back sanely (CAPLENGTH 0x30, HCIVERSION
0x0110, one port with power control) and a hand-poked HCRESET clears immediately
and leaves USBCMD at the 0x00080000 reset default. What is missing is host-mode
bring-up: this core is an OTG part with Moorestown-style per-port HOSTPC
registers, and until ehci->has_hostpc is set the driver drives it through the
wrong register path, it never enters host mode, and the reset times out.

Intel's GPL CE5300 BSP handles exactly this under CONFIG_GEN3_USB: for device
0x0101 rev >= 6 it sets has_hostpc, and (separately) clears has_tdi_phy_lpm
because the PHY has no usable link-power-management. This folds both into the
existing PCI_VENDOR_ID_TDI quirk. has_hostpc also fixes PORTSC line-status
decoding (ehci.h uses bit 25 vs 26 depending on it), which is what lets a
connected device -- e.g. the EM357 Zigbee module's CP2104 bridge on controller
01:0d.2 -- actually enumerate instead of sitting with CCS=0.

Without this there is no /dev/ttyUSB*, so neither the Zigbee radio nor any other
USB peripheral can come up.
---
--- a/drivers/usb/host/ehci-pci.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/ehci-pci.c
@@ -145,8 +145,23 @@
 			hcd->has_tt = 1;
 		break;
 	case PCI_VENDOR_ID_TDI:
-		if (pdev->device == PCI_DEVICE_ID_TDI_EHCI)
+		if (pdev->device == PCI_DEVICE_ID_TDI_EHCI) {
 			hcd->has_tt = 1;
+			/*
+			 * openHC: the TransDimension EHCI on the Intel CE5300/
+			 * CE2600 (Control4 EA, 192e:0101 rev >= 6) is an OTG core
+			 * with Moorestown-style per-port HOSTPC registers. Without
+			 * has_hostpc it never enters host mode and ehci_setup()'s
+			 * reset handshake times out (-110), killing every USB port.
+			 * Its PHY also has no working LPM. Both quirks come from
+			 * Intel's GPL CE5300 BSP (the CONFIG_GEN3_USB ehci-pci
+			 * changes) -- see board/ea-common/patches/linux/README.md.
+			 */
+			if (pdev->revision >= 0x6) {
+				ehci->has_hostpc = 1;
+				ehci->has_tdi_phy_lpm = 0;
+			}
+		}
 		break;
 	case PCI_VENDOR_ID_AMD:
 		/* AMD PLL quirk */
